---
title: "Taliesin"
description: "The user guide: how to write, preview, execute and publish a .tmd document, from your first preview to a deployed site."
---

# Why Taliesin {.unnumbered}

**A fast, single-purpose dev server that turns `.tmd` files into live HTML (blog
posts, papers, books, and whole websites) with executable code blocks and an
editing loop that updates the instant you save.**

Taliesin is built around one idea: writing technical content should feel as
immediate as editing code. You save the file, and only the block you changed
re-renders: your scroll position, your live 3D canvas, your warm kernel all stay
exactly as they were. No cold start, no full-page flash, no waiting.

## Three things it gets right

- **Click-to-source.** Every rendered block remembers the line it came from.
  Ctrl-click (Cmd-click on Mac) anything in the preview to jump straight to its
  source, even across `{{< include >}}`d files.
- **Block-level incremental updates.** A save re-renders only the blocks that
  changed. Live canvases (Three.js, `{js}` cells) keep their state; the
  page never reloads.
- **No per-edit startup cost.** A warm server and a warm Jupyter kernel mean
  edits show up immediately, and only the cells whose code actually changed
  re-run.

This guide is itself a multi-page **book** built with Taliesin: the Chapters drawer, search
(press <kbd>Cmd/Ctrl-K</kbd>), live diagrams, and executed code cells included. A
companion book, [Internals](../internals/), covers how it works inside.

## The 60-second version {.unnumbered}

Write a `.tmd` file, run `taliesin preview` on it, and leave the browser open beside
your editor. Every save re-renders only the block you touched, so a figure a `{python}`
cell drew stays on screen while you edit the paragraph above it, and Ctrl-clicking
(Cmd-clicking on Mac) any block in the preview opens its source line in your editor.
That loop is the whole product; everything below is what you can put inside it.

## Coming from Jupyter?

Taliesin reads the Markdown/Pandoc `.tmd` syntax you already know, but it makes a
particular trade: **do one thing, author HTML, and make the edit loop instant.**

- vs **Jupyter**: you write in plain text files (versionable, diffable) with real
  prose, math, citations, and cross-references (not notebook JSON) while still
  getting live, cached, in-place code execution.

It is intentionally small: one author's HTML workflow, kept fast.

## Get running

taliesin builds from source with a Rust toolchain (`cargo build --release`) — a
first build takes a few minutes; after that the binary is warm. Put
`target/release/taliesin` on your `PATH` (a symlink, or a small wrapper script that
rebuilds on source changes before running it) so you can call it from
anywhere. To execute code cells you also point it at a Jupyter kernel: see
[Executable content](using/code.tmd#code-cells).

```sh
# live preview, updating as you save
cargo run -p taliesin-server -- preview post.tmd 4321

# build a self-contained HTML file (code cells are executed)
cargo run -p taliesin-server -- build post.tmd

# point either command at a directory to get a whole multi-page site
cargo run -p taliesin-server -- preview blog/
```

Open `http://127.0.0.1:4321`, edit, and watch a single block update in place with
your scroll preserved. The server binds to loopback only.

One block model drives every output, a standalone page, a site page, or a book chapter:

```{mermaid}
flowchart LR
  Q[".tmd"] --> BM["block model"]
  BM --> H["HTML page"]
  BM --> S["website + nav"]
  BM --> K["book + TOC"]
```

## What it is (and isn't)

::: {.callout-note}
## Scope

taliesin renders to **HTML only**: there is no PDF path and no format matrix. It
is a focused tool for one author's writing workflow. The "spec" is a small corpus
of real documents: when those render
correctly, the tool is done. That focus is the point: it stays fast, small, and
predictable.
:::

The rest of this guide tours what you can put on a page: [the live preview
loop](using/preview.tmd), [executable content](using/code.tmd) (Python cells,
`{js}` cells, 3D graphics), [rich writing](using/writing.tmd) (math, diagrams,
figures, citations), and [recipes for books and sites](using/recipes.tmd). For how
Taliesin works inside, read the companion [Internals](../internals/) book.
